### How these arrays represent directions
We only ever move either horizontally (left/right) or vertically (up/down), so we never change both row and column at the same time (no diagonals here). That’s why half the values are 0:
- If moving horizontally: row stays the same → `dRow = 0`, column changes
- If moving vertically: column stays the same → `dCol = 0`, row changes

Let’s walk through each index `k` (we only ever use `k = 0,1,2,3`, more on that later) to see what movement it gives us, using `newI = i + dRow[k]` and `newJ = j + dCol[k]`:

| k | dRow[k] | dCol[k] | What happens to position | Direction |
|---|---------|---------|--------------------------|-----------|
| 0 | 0       | 1       | Same row, column +1      | Right     |
| 1 | 0       | -1      | Same row, column -1      | Left      |
| 2 | 1       | 0       | Row +1, same column      | Down      |
| 3 | -1      | 0       | Row -1, same column      | Up        |

That’s exactly the four directions we need! Let’s test this with your example grid’s top-left cell `(i=0, j=0)` (value 1):
- k=0 (right): newI=0, newJ=1 → cell (0,1) = 3 (valid, >1)
- k=1 (left): newI=0, newJ=-1 → out of bounds (skip)
- k=2 (down): newI=1, newJ=0 → cell (1,0) = 2 (valid, >1)
- k=3 (up): newI=-1, newJ=0 → out of bounds (skip)

This is exactly the two valid moves we’d expect from that cell!

